      "dom_id": "question-53062",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. A square is sometimes not a rectangle.\n\nII. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral.\n\nIII. A square is never a rhombus.",
      "worked_solution": "First, recall the hierarchy diagram of quadrilaterals, where each shape below another is a specific type of the one above it.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191275/assets/1dbe4c3e8ea97c0ac077c37143802366c5817d86965ab1dbc5abf54cd01d27c3.png)\n\nLet's now look at each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. In a rectangle, its four angles are right angles. The same is true for a square. A square is a specific type of rectangle, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\n- Statement II is true. A rectangle is always a quadrilateral because it has four sides.\n\n- Statement III is false. A rhombus is a parallelogram where all sides are congruent. A square is a specific type of rhombus, where the four angles are right angles and the four sides are congruent.\n\nIn conclusion, only statement II is true.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
